The nation shattered, yet the mountains and rivers remain;
Spring fills the ruined city, grass and trees grow deep.
Moved by the troubled times, flowers bring tears to my eyes;
Grieving over parting, birdsong startles my heart.
Beacon fires have burned three months without cease;
A letter from home would be worth ten thousand gold.
I scratch my whitening hair, thinner now with every touch—
It can scarcely hold a pin in place.

