The wind is sharp, the sky is high, where gibbons keen and cry;
The isles run clear, the sands run white, birds wheel against the sky.

The boundless woods shed leaves that hiss and fall without an end;
The endless Yangtze rolls and surges ceaselessly, without end.

Ten thousand miles away, each autumn I am still a guest;
A whole life long, grown old and sick, I climb this height alone.

My troubled years, my bitter griefs have bleached my temples white;
I've sunk so low that I've but now given up the murky wine.

