The nation is shattered—mountains and rivers remain;
Spring fills the city—grass and trees grow deep.

Moved by the times, flowers draw forth tears;
Grieving over parting, birds startle the heart.

Beacon fires have burned three months unbroken;
A letter from home would be worth ten thousand gold.

I scratch my whitening hair—it grows ever thinner,
Soon too scant to hold a simple pin.

