Ascending the Heights

The wind is sharp, the sky is high, gibbons cry with sorrow;
The islet pure, the sand white, birds wheel and circle round.
Boundless, the falling leaves descend with rustling sound;
Endless, the Yangtze rolls its waves with roaring sound.

Ten thousand miles away, I grieve at autumn, often a wanderer;
A hundred years of life, beset by illness, I climb this terrace alone.
Hardship and bitter regret have thickened my frosty temples;
In my decline, I have lately ceased my cup of turbid wine.

