**Climbing High**

Sharp wind, high sky, the gibbons’ mournful cry;
Clear islet, white sand, birds wheel and circle by.
Boundless, the falling leaves rustle to the ground;
Endless, the rolling Yangtze surges on without sound.
Ten thousand miles of autumn grief, I roam and roam;
A hundred years of illness, I climb alone to this home.
Hardship and bitter hate have thickened my hoary hair;
Down and out, I’ve just stopped my cup of turbid liquor there.

