When the nation falls, mountains and rivers remain;  
In the spring city, grass and trees grow deep.  
Sorrowing for the times, flowers shed tears;  
Hating parting, birds alarm the heart.  
War flames burn for three months;  
A home letter is worth ten thousand gold.  
My white hair, scratched, grows shorter still;  
I can barely hold my hairpin.

